  "query_preview": "..Remnant low level moisture remains locked in place\nacross the region, as is evident with the extent of low level\ncloud cover revealed in satellite imagery. That said, the rapid\ndecrease in mid- and high-level cloud cover tells the better story\nof the amount of dry air filtering into the region on the back\nside of the recent storm system. Lower level cloud cover will\ndecrease as night falls this evening, allowing for strong\nradiational cooling across the region, with many areas dropping\ninto the 30s or even below freezing (focus here will be in the\nhigher elevation valley areas). With the amount of moisture\nrecieved over the weekend and saturation of the soils across the\nregion, strong radiational cooling is likely to allow for\ndevelopment of fog. Areas of particular concern for fog\ndevelopment will be in west-central and southwest Utah, as well as\nthe Cache Valley. This fog is not expected to persist into the\nafternoon hours on Tuesday as the drier airmass being introduced\nwill help to mix drier air to the surface during the heating\nhours.\n\nDry and cool conditions will persist through the day on Tuesday,\nwith high temperatures running 5 to 10 degrees below normal for\nthis time of year. High level cloud cover will accompany these\ncooler temperatures, making it feel a bit colder given the lack of\nsolar insolation reaching the surface (especially for far northern\nand southern Utah). \n\nThe overall pattern remains relatively active, as another area of\nlow pressure looks to progress through the region from Wednesday\ninto Thursday. The most available moisture associated with this\nnext area of low pressure will exist over central and northern\nUtah where valley rain and snowfall above 7,000-7,500ft will be\nexpected. Overall moisture content will be lacking with this\nsystem as it is being sourced from the PacNW region instead of the\nsubtropics like our past large systems have. With that in mind,\nstorm total precipitation is expected to range from just a few\nhundredths to around a quarter inch in the valleys of northern and\ncentral Utah, with mountains seeing a fairly similar range. For\nsnowfall, elevations above around 7,500-8,000ft are expected to\nsee anywhere from around a half inch to upwards of 3-4 inches\n(particularly in the upper Cottonwoods and the north slopes of the\nUinta Mountains). Big picture: this next system will bring very\nlimited impacts to the region and is not expected to bring any\nconcern for flooding.\n\nThereafter, dry conditions will filter back into the region late\nin the week and through the weekend as high pressure reestablishes\nacross Utah and southwest Wyoming. A gradual warming trend can be\nexpected from Thursday through at least Monday, with temperatures\nwarming back to near, to slightly above, normal for this time of\nyear by early next week.",
